---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Used from "../components/Used.astro";
import { tokens } from "../lib/tokens";
import { palettes } from "../lib/palettes";

const modeAware = tokens.filter((t) => t.modeAware).length;

const accentBundle =
  `/* brand-accent.css: re-declares the complete semantic bundle. */\n:root {\n` +
  `  --color-action-accent: light-dark(${palettes.blue[700]}, ${palettes.blue[400]});\n` +
  `  --color-text-accent: light-dark(${palettes.blue[700]}, ${palettes.blue[300]});\n` +
  `  --color-border-accent: light-dark(${palettes.blue[600]}, ${palettes.blue[500]});\n` +
  `  --color-bg-accent-subtle: light-dark(${palettes.blue[50]}, ${palettes.blue[950]});\n` +
  "}";

const densityUse = `/* A multiplier, not a second scale. */
html { --sk-density: 0.8; }`;

const radiusUse = `<!-- A preset of the multiplier… -->
<html data-radius="xl">

<!-- …or a continuous value off the preset ladder. -->
<html style="--radius-multiplier: 1.25">`;
---

<Base
  title="Four dimensions"
  documentLayout="grid"
  description="Color mode, high contrast, density and radius: four axes that compose in the cascade. Brand re-declares semantic bundles."
>
  <h1>Four dimensions</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Color mode, high contrast, density and radius: four independent axes that <strong>compose in the cascade</strong> rather than being enumerated on disk.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tier 1 palettes are meaning-free raw material. A brand does not pick a semantic bundle from a list: it
    re-declares the semantic accent bundle on <code>:root</code>, and every other token keeps its meaning.
    There is no brand catalogue, selector, or attribute to maintain inside Core.
  </p>

  <h2>Each dimension has a different layer</h2>
  <p>That is the whole reason they compose instead of multiply.</p>

  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead class="sk-table__head">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <th class="sk-table__header">Dimension</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">Owns</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">Mechanism</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">Turned on with</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="sk-table__body">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><strong>Color mode</strong></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">colour, tier 2</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><code>light-dark()</code> picks the slot</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">color-scheme</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><strong>High contrast</strong></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">colour, tier 2</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">re-declares the same tokens, in stronger pairs</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">data-contrast</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><strong>Density</strong></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">spacing, tier 2</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">a runtime multiplier, with the floor inside it</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">--sk-density</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><strong>Radius</strong></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">roundness, tier 2</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">a preset, or a runtime multiplier</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell mono">data-radius / --radius-multiplier</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
    <p>
      <strong>The separation remains structural:</strong> palettes are tier 1 and semantic tokens name
      meaning. Replacing the accent never steps on mode, contrast, density, or radius: every axis stays
      independent.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>


  <h2>Accent, an explicit bundle</h2>
  <p>
    <code>tokens.scss</code> includes public palettes and default semantics. Your branding tool generates
    layerless CSS that replaces the complete semantic bundle; Core does not receive a root color or derive
    combinations at runtime. This example shows part of the accent bundle.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={accentBundle} label="brand-accent.css" />

  <h2>Color mode, two slots, and only two</h2>
  <p>
    <code>light-dark()</code> takes <strong>exactly two</strong> arguments, and is only valid where a <code>&lt;color&gt;</code> is valid. Neither restriction is worked around here; both are accepted, and between them they shape the rest of the model.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Three modes do not fit in two slots</strong>, so high contrast is not a third argument: it is
      a separate override block that re-declares the same tokens, still inside
      <code>light-dark()</code>, because it composes with the light/dark axis.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What is not a colour cannot be mode-aware.</strong> A shadow, a length, a font family: none of
      them can ever live inside <code>light-dark()</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Which is why <strong>every mode-aware token in the system is a colour token</strong>, all {modeAware} of them, and why elevation is modelled as colour plus a composite rather than as a shadow per mode. That follows from the platform, not from taste.
  </p>

  <h2>Density, a multiplier with the floor inside</h2>
  <p>
    A single number, <code>--sk-density</code> (1 is comfortable). There is no second spacing scale, only
    this one multiplying the first. It is continuous, so any value works, and the <strong>controls
    above</strong> offer five presets as shortcuts, from <em>spacious</em> (1.2) to
    <em>condensed</em> (0.5).
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={densityUse} label="CSS" />

  <p>
    To change one container alone, add <code>data-sk-density-scope</code>. Its <code>--sk-density-factor</code> multiplies the system density, while a <code>--sk-density</code> written into the scope sets an absolute value instead. The presets are only convenient names; any valid number still works.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/density">The component density guide</a> shows that local scope on a real component, and when
    to choose a relative factor over an absolute value.
  </p>

  <p>
    Set the density to <em>dense</em> above and watch these two:
  </p>
  <div class="sk-box" data-surface="surface" data-border="subtle" data-padding="lg">
    <p>
      <code>--space-inset-md</code> → <Used token="--space-inset-md" /><br />
      <code>--size-control-md</code> → <Used token="--size-control-md" />
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
    <p>
      <strong>The accessibility floor is inside the token, not in the documentation. </strong> Interactive sizes resolve to <code>max(round(calc(base × density), 2px), 24px)</code>. That <code>max()</code> is the WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.8 minimum: no team can multiply their way below 24px, whatever they assign to <code>--sk-density</code>. The floor holds by construction, not by code review.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The <code>round(…, 2px)</code> is not decoration either: <code>calc(4px * 0.6)</code> is 2.4px, which falls off the pixel grid, and a handful of those together make a compact UI look unsteady. At density 1 it does nothing at all.
  </p>

  <h3>What is deliberately density invariant?</h3>
  <p>
    Typography, because scaling font-size without correcting line-height reads worse, and density is a
    spacing concern rather than a type one. Corner radius, because roundness is its own dimension. Focus ring
    width, because a ring that shrinks stops being visible. And the touch area, which is allowed to paint
    small while still responding large.
  </p>

  <h3>Touch target, 44px no matter how small it paints</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--size-touch-target</code> is a fixed value, <strong>44px</strong> (iOS HIG), that does not
    respond to density. Button and Input consume it with
    <code>max(control-size, size-touch-target)</code>: the control can never end up smaller than 44px
    no matter how compact the size scale gets, but it can still paint larger when its own visual size
    already clears that floor (<code>lg</code>, for instance).
  </p>
  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
      <p>
        <strong>Painted small, hit area large.</strong> An icon-only button at <code>sm</code> paints
        at its real visual size, smaller than 44px: but an invisible <code>::after</code> extends the
        clickable area up to the touch floor without growing the control on screen. It's the same
        pattern RadioGroup uses (<code>--sk-radio-hit-size</code>, a capture circle larger than the
        visible dot), and Switch solves it differently: there, the whole label is the touch area, so
        the visual track doesn't need to carry the floor on its own.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    17 components under <code>packages/core/css/components</code> consume the token today, among them
    Combobox, Select, DatePicker and CommandPalette. Any new interactive control should join that
    list rather than invent its own minimum.
  </p>

  <h2>Radius, roundness as an axis, not as a brand value</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>multiplier</strong>, not one flat value for everything: <code>--radius-multiplier</code> scales
    a ladder where each role keeps its place, so <code>control</code> and <code>surface</code> stay distinct at
    any setting. It borrows the density mechanism, not the high contrast one. The five presets under
    <code>data-radius</code> (<code>none 0 · sm .5 · md 1 · lg 1.5 · xl 2</code>, with <code>md</code> as the
    base) set only that multiplier, and a product can pin a continuous value off the ladder
    (<code>1.25</code>, say). It is orthogonal to the brand: the same brand reads lively or soft without a
    single palette change.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={radiusUse} label="HTML" />

  <p>
    Change the radius above and look at the two roles that move:
  </p>
  <div class="sk-box" data-surface="surface" data-border="subtle" data-padding="lg">
    <p>
      <code>--radius-control</code> → <Used token="--radius-control" /><br />
      <code>--radius-surface</code> → <Used token="--radius-surface" />
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
    <p>
      <strong>The dimension's invariant: <code>--radius-pill</code> does not move.</strong> The axis moves the
      two roles of roundness, control and surface, and leaves the shape alone: a pill is a fully round lozenge,
      not a degree of roundness, and squaring it off at <code>none</code> would erase what it is. Every
      dimension carries its own invariant inside it: density has the WCAG floor, radius has the pill.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <p>
    A dimension is not just any product preference. If an axis does not own a layer of the token graph, it is
    modelled as a semantic token, a styling hook, or a component, never as another global dimension.
  </p>
  <p>
    This follows <a href="/tiers">Tiers</a>, which is where the reason these overrides live in the semantic
    tier is set out. It continues in <a href="/density">Density</a>, which takes the global axis down to local
    scopes, and in <a href="/elevation">Elevation</a>, which shows what a colour dimension does to a composed
    scale.
  </p>
</Base>
